  CONTROL OK   a planted suppressed address shows EXCLUDED with its reason
           planted  blocked-control@example.test added to a populated temporary agency-DNC file through agencydnc.add
           expected ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 'EXCLUDED', True]
           observed ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 'EXCLUDED', True]
  CONTROL OK   a planted contact whose store read raises shows UNKNOWN and the run exits non-zero
           planted  the queue authority's resolver raising OSError
           expected ['PRESENT_UNREADABLE', 'UNKNOWN', 2]
           observed ['PRESENT_UNREADABLE', 'UNKNOWN', 2]
  CONTROL OK   a planted clean contact shows PASS on every rule that can be asked
           planted  clean-control@example.invalid against present, POPULATED, readable authority files, each holding a filler record that matches nobody
           expected ['PASS', 'PASS', 'PASS', 'PASS']
           observed ['PASS', 'PASS', 'PASS', 'PASS']
  CONTROL OK   a present-and-EMPTY authority yields UNKNOWN, not PASS, and the run exits non-zero
           planted  an agency-DNC file that exists, opens and parses to zero records, with every other authority populated and the contact's record supplied
           expected ['PRESENT_EMPTY', 0, 'UNKNOWN', True]
           observed ['PRESENT_EMPTY', 0, 'UNKNOWN', True]
           NOTE     THE CELL IS THE DISCRIMINATOR HERE, NOT THE EXIT CODE: other rules are UNKNOWN for their own reasons, so this run exits 2 either way. Weaken the state check so PRESENT_EMPTY maps to PRESENT_POPULATED and the agency_dnc cell becomes PASS, which is what this row catches.
  CONTROL OK   a POPULATED authority returns a real verdict rather than UNKNOWN
           planted  the same agency-DNC authority as control 4, holding exactly ONE record: the synthetic blocked-control@example.test
           expected ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 1, 'EXCLUDED']
           observed ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 1, 'EXCLUDED']
           NOTE     EXISTS BECAUSE CONTROL 4 AND THE EXIT CODE ARE BOTH PASSED BY AN INSTRUMENT THAT ANSWERS UNKNOWN TO EVERYTHING. One record versus none is the only difference between this row and that one.
  CONTROL OK   a planted member of a DECLARED INTERNAL campaign is EXCLUDED, with the campaign named
           planted  member-control@example.test given a lead row in campaign 999001, which a planted declaration names internal
           expected ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 'EXCLUDED', True, 1]
           observed ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 'EXCLUDED', True, 1]
           NOTE     THE CAMPAIGN NUMBER IS PART OF THE OBSERVATION. An exclusion that cannot say which campaign is an exclusion nobody can check, and the headline moving to `1 EXCLUDED` is the fourth cell because that is the sentence a reader acts on.
  CONTROL OK   a planted NON-member is PASS against a listing that proves its walk complete
           planted  clean-control@example.invalid absent from a populated listing whose every internal campaign records leads_reported == leads_read
           expected ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 'PASS']
           observed ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 'PASS']
           NOTE     EXISTS BECAUSE CONTROL 6 IS PASSED BY A RULE THAT ANSWERS UNKNOWN TO EVERYBODY. It is also the proof that PASS is reachable at all for this rule - today it is reachable only against a planted listing, because the real producer discards the provider's own total and so cannot prove a walk complete.
  CONTROL OK   a RECORDED bounce in the queue authority is EXCLUDED with the event named, not UNKNOWN
           planted  already-bounced-control@example.test given ONE queue record carrying an EMAIL_BOUNCED event, read back through store.read_jsonl
           expected ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 1, 'EXCLUDED', True, 1]
           observed ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 1, 'EXCLUDED', True, 1]
           NOTE     THE EVENT NAME IS PART OF THE OBSERVATION, and so is the headline moving to `1 EXCLUDED`. Before TASK-0250 this row observed UNKNOWN: the authority was populated, the record was supplied, the bounce was in it, and the cell said the rule had no form that could be evaluated.
  CONTROL OK   the same record with NO bounce event is UNKNOWN naming the recorder, not PASS
           planted  the control-8 plant with an EMPTY events list - exactly what an unrecorded bounce looks like
           expected ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 1, 'UNKNOWN', True]
           observed ['PRESENT_POPULATED', 1, 'UNKNOWN', True]
           NOTE     EXISTS BECAUSE CONTROL 8 IS PASSED BY A RULE THAT EXCLUDES EVERYBODY. One event versus none is the only difference between this row and that one, and `channels._bounced` answers False here - so a cell reading PASS would be reporting that `False` as a fact about the address.
